  "details": "In talking with friends who visit public worlds, I've been told that it's possible for things like malicious shaders, scripts that bypass the SDK and run on load, and particles have been used as crash vectors before that have required them to use unofficial clients to protect themselves from.\nTo combat this, I believe a serverside verification process should be used. This would combine two things:\n1. On upload, your avatar is queued for processing, during this time you can switch to it, but others will see the previous version or your fallback.\n2. Serverside the avatar is unpacked and scanned for content that should definitely not be there, such as scripts or invalid components. If present, your update is aborted and you are notified via the game, SDK and website. This can be processed fairly quickly on a cloud server.\n3. If valid, your avatar enters a 'safe but unverified state'. Friends and those who choose to can see your new avatar which will automatically switch if you are still wearing it but others won't.\n4. Your avatar is then moved in to a slower stage two verification process and is loaded by a sandboxed server running a special copy of VRC that will automatically cycle through all animation states, variable states and so on within the avatar and monitor the application. It crashes or significantly hangs, your avatar will be set as failed and will revert back to disabled. This would probably take a little longer which is why being friends would bypass this check.\n5. If the avatar causes temporary performance issues (way beyond 'Very Poor'), rapid high contrast (epilepsy risk) colour changes or other undesirable but not banned behaviour, it will be marked as 'risky' and others will be notified if they try and show your avatar but it will be unblocked.\n6. If no issues are found the avatar is marked as verified and all users will now be able to see it (and their clients will automatically update to fetch it as before), subject to their own safety settings.\nOther considerations: uploading a new version would restart this process, likewise the server processing costs would mean users would likely only be able to have one avatar in the queue at the time. Uploading multiple avatars at once would mean each one would be made to wait and not placed in the global queue until their first avatar exits verification so that one user can't fill the queue with lots of uploads.",
